\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Three simulated trials. The tally starts at zero and wanders; the walk stops when it first touches a bound. The rust trace has a slight upward drift and ends at "tiger"; the blue trace drifts down and ends quickly at "breeze"; the grey trace has almost no drift, wanders a long time, and only eventually commits. Weak evidence means slow decisions.}}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {7}Priors: Building In What We Already Know}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Left: with equal priors the crossing point sits between the two peaks. Right: when the signal is rare the $+$ curve shrinks, the crossing point slides right, and the threshold becomes far more conservative. The distributions themselves are unchanged --- only their weights.}}{9}{}\protected@file@percent }
